(*
Issue: a record field whose own type is another record, nested
arbitrarily deep (record f: record f: record f: ... end end end),
recurses through parseTypeExpr with no depth limit, so before this test
existed it exhausted the real C++ stack instead of failing cleanly
(BUG A, same funnel point as the other two type-nesting cases in this
directory).  500 levels already reaches the 500-deep ceiling
(MaxTypeDepth, ParseType.cpp) while staying fast to parse here -- each
level here is far heavier to parse than one '(' or one 'array[1..1] of',
since a record field also registers a name in VarNames_ and parses a
':' and a nested record header, so a smaller level count than the other
two files in this directory is used to keep this file's build-time cost
reasonable while still safely exceeding the ceiling.

Checked in verbatim rather than generated at build time -- lit has no
loop-emitting primitive, and the source is deterministic; nobody should
have to look at 500 levels of 'record f: ... end' to read this file, so
the lit directives come first.

RUN: not %plang_ir -dump-parse-tree %s 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: error: type is nested too deeply
*)

